# .public-surface-deny - denials this repository adds on top of the fleet
# baseline in scripts/public-surface-check. Same four fields as
# .public-surface-allow:
#
#     category | path-glob | reason | pattern
#
# The reason is printed as the remediation text when the rule fires, so write
# it as an instruction. Patterns use %HH hex escapes so this file does not
# spell the names it denies; the guard scans this file too.
#
# These carry over the deny list of the previous repository-local guard.
# Invariant Protocol is published as a general-purpose protocol framework: it
# must read as its own project, not as a component of a product, so the
# sibling product brand, the organisation brand token, and the private tool
# name stay off this surface.
#
# One entry of the old deny list is deliberately NOT carried over: the sibling
# framework repository this one is often mentioned next to is itself public, so
# naming it leaks nothing and a cross-reference between two public repositories
# is allowed. The fleet baseline already treats it as a public sibling.

brand-isolation | * | Invariant Protocol is published as a standalone framework. Name the capability, not the sibling product brand. | (^|[^a-z0-9_])%6dedallion($|[^a-z0-9_])
brand-isolation | * | The organisation brand token belongs on product surfaces, not in this framework's docs or code. Public package coordinates are allowed already. | (^|[^a-z0-9_.-])%6aimtech($|[^a-z0-9_.-])
brand-isolation | * | This names a private internal tool. Describe the storage or transport boundary instead. | (^|[^a-z0-9_])%67hdrive($|[^a-z0-9_])
